Desmond, Rosalind,<br />
<br />
Preliminary analysis from the fieldwork sample is attached. The finding tha=
t matters is this one.<br />
<br />
Marchwood's advisory agreement for 214 of its 1,140 accounts gives the advi=
ser, at Section 6(c), standing authority to instruct the client's custodian=
 to withdraw or transfer funds or securities from the account, including bu=
t not limited to amounts due to Marchwood as advisory fees. In practice Mar=
chwood has used the authority only to debit its fees, but the authority con=
ferred is not limited to fees. Marchwood transmits the instruction itself a=
nd the custodian executes it without separate client authorization. Under R=
ule 206(4)-2 as amended effective March 12, 2010, that authority is custody=
, and because it is not confined to the advisory fee the exception at parag=
raph (b)(3) is not available. Marchwood answered Items 9.A.(1)(a) and 9.A.(=
1)(b) of Form ADV Part 1 "no".<br />
<br />
Marchwood therefore did not obtain the surprise examination required by Rul=
e 206(4)-2(a)(4) for calendar year 2010 and has not engaged an independent =
public accountant for 2011. No Form ADV-E has been filed. The accounts in q=
uestion hold approximately $198 million of the firm's $612 million in regul=
atory assets under management.<br />
<br />
The other five items in the draft are routine - a stale compliance manual, =
an incomplete code of ethics reporting log, two advertising items and a boo=
ks-and-records retention gap. Item 4 is the one with teeth.<br />
<br />
Priya Raghunathan<br />
Examiner, Philadelphia Regional Office
